#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549274 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=6 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=6 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=6\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549277 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) \\\\ 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) \\\\ 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=3\\left({e}^{x}\\right),\\,v(x,y,z)=3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right),\\,w(x,y,z)=2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScaling the three derivative rows by $3, 3, 2$ multiplies the determinant by $18$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0)\\right)\\\\  & =18 e^{x} e^{x + y} e^{x + y + z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18\\left({e}^{3x+2y+z}\\right) & =18\\left({e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{v}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{w}{2}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u v w}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549282 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 4 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-4 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-4\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-4\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549289 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 5 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-5 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-5\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-5\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549979 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}xz⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+bu{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-u)+{u}^{2}v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =-2u{v}^{2}-u{v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-3u{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\cdot {u}^{2}{v}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2-3=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550122 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{\\pi}{3})=(2,\\frac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550125 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550126 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{2\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=(2,\\frac{2\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550139 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(4, θ)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2, θ - \\frac{π}{4})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{8},-\\sqrt{8})$." :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\   & =\\operatorname{arctan}(1) \\\\   & =\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we have $\\theta=\\frac{7\\pi}{4}.$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2,\\frac{7\\pi}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right))=(2,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(0) \\\\   & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(-1) \\\\   & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550150 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{8},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(16,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-2}{-2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(16,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(16,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(16,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550151 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{5\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{5\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550154 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{7\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{7\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\   & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550166 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{12},-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-2}{-\\sqrt{12}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(8,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550175 :question/problem "![](images/44/44f13c74646a1a3dda14673314c545a5909a72b05ba9f967ebcaff36f7388b76.png)\n\nThe parabola shown above has equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=12x.\n$$\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=12x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550181 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1617023328f096064631df2281e8a3c2eeb471ee4676f937226eb0d1da378d8.png)\n\nThe parabola shown above has equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=2x.\n$$\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550186 :question/problem "![](images/61/61d540bfd94984bf7a96a5981ca65233399beec607d469fc1641b2326826a4d4.png)\n\nThe parabola shown above has equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=25x.\n$$\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=25x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{25}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550187 :question/problem "![](images/a7/a7c56fd2bfe1aa346bcebdf79f128140235c69fd72cf5b147eace57827fe9689.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-6,-3).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-6$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 9 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{3}{2}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{3}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550198 :question/problem "![](images/00/0012f2c4830f5acc401b551e8421b0bbb730c2e27f68010a691d74dcfd6ea93a.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(6,-3).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=6$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 9 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550201 :question/problem "![](images/03/03a879d7f79141f6816bf74a73efa87b089e2c213bc2b58bc93727cd8ee08500.png)\n\nThe parabola shown above has equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x.\n$$\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550203 :question/problem "![](images/19/19065a69504e6bd90d36a7804f05ce9952a90c838c1ddb43c374e53ccbf038a7.png)\n\nThe parabola shown above has equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=1x.\n$$\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=1x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550214 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(4,0)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $7.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,0),$ $a=3$ and $b=7.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=7\\sin\\theta,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550215 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(-1,2)$ with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $6.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=4$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta-1,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta+2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550227 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(4,-2)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,-2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta-2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550233 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-6)$ with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $3.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,-6),$ $a=7$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta-6,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550243 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(4,3)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $4.$ Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta+h,\\,y=b\\sin\\theta+k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,3),$ $a=3$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+4,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta+3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550250 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=4 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{2}{5 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{7}{4 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{2}{5 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=5 & ⟹\\, & u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d3/d3dd7067bbcb0a6e3c901d12a2795500e192594a14e7f1373dd325e4fd270363.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550274 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=2 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{3}{x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 6,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/15/15f5caab85f05d666bb383692bad6b67d04da853f1412505a62ba6aba7ce36d6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{8 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{8 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{8 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{6}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 6-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550281 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\qquad v=x y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=3 x^{2},\\qquad y=7 x^{2},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{8}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\,v=x y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{y}{x^{2}}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=7 x^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{y}{x^{2}}=7 & ⟹\\, & u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{8}{x} & ⟹\\, & x y=8 & ⟹\\, & v=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,1\\le v\\le 8\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/6c/6cd1c5b38643b6c5e0ceded7b3cae3d06e51135f84fb9598a129e8627b55c14a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2 y}{x^{3}} & \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 y}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{x^{2}}{3 y}=- \\frac{1}{3 u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3 u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{7}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{3 u}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{3}^{7}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{8}1\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 3)\\cdot (8-1) \\\\  & =- \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{7 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550282 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\qquad v=3 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{x},\\qquad y=\\frac{3}{8 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{5}{3 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=3 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{5}{3 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 3 x^{3} y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 8,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/80/80a923224b455c6f8cfa17a7099c1bb86206a115845954af6123bfb6b3ef5138.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 9 x^{2} y & 3 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{18 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{18 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{18 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{8}\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{8}+1)\\cdot (\\ln 5-\\ln 3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{16} + \\frac{21 \\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550289 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=4 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x - 2,\\qquad y=2 x + 2,\\qquad y=2 - 4 x,\\qquad y=4 - 4 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=4 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/434447c0d9ece418e17a0ac3f0cc044faf76ba5689ed692fc3408cb78501326c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550292 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{x y},\\qquad v=x^{2} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{8 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{4}{x^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{x y},\\,v=x^{2} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{8 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x^{2} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x^{2}} & ⟹\\, & x^{2} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{1}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 2 x y & x^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=y=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{x^{2} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550299 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x,\\qquad y=2 x + 2,\\qquad y=3 - x,\\qquad y=4 - x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 2 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=4 - x & ⟹\\, & x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,3\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2c3cb5e8b7c7448b5f90e372e20e6ff2bd0e547dd085d34ac3103178083ce8c2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{3}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550308 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\qquad v=4 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{x y},\\,v=4 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{2}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{2}{x y}=8 & ⟹\\, & u=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{4 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 4 x^{3} y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f506bb81b18dce423e50eedc19841b7a5896230413f7f1a876aa93d27e68edf9.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{2}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 12 x^{2} y & 4 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{16 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{16 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{16 x^{3} y}=\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\int_{3}^{8}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (\\ln 4-\\ln 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550309 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\qquad v=2 x^{3} y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{3 x},\\qquad y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}},\\qquad y=\\frac{7}{2 x^{3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{x y},\\,v=2 x^{3} y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{3}{x y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{3}{4 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3 x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{3}{x y}=9 & ⟹\\, & u=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2 x^{3} y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{7}{2 x^{3}} & ⟹\\, & 2 x^{3} y=7 & ⟹\\, & v=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 9,\\,2\\le v\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/07/075e4196e1ad4b6dafc70878555598bac64e8880b2c152d61c2a6437acbaaceb.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3}{x^{2} y} & - \\frac{3}{x y^{2}} \\\\ 6 x^{2} y & 2 x^{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{12 x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{12 x}=\\frac{x^{2} y^{2}}{12 x^{3} y}=\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\int_{4}^{9}\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{3}{2 u^{2} v}\\,\\mathrm d v\\,\\mathrm d u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm d u\\int_{2}^{7}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\mathrm d v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (\\ln 7-\\ln 2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{24} + \\frac{5 \\ln{\\left(7 \\right)}}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550311 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=3 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x - 4,\\qquad y=2 x + 4,\\qquad y=3 - 3 x,\\qquad y=5 - 3 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=3 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x - 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=-4 & ⟹\\, & u=-4 \\\\  & y=2 x + 4 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=4 & ⟹\\, & u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3 \\\\  & y=5 - 3 x & ⟹\\, & 3 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/43/438f7c0ee42f63a596ff65ae9c2b354c93bb2f97ad4d7b5b0a1a161a3c5d173b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{5}=- \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550313 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\qquad v=4 x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=2 x,\\qquad y=2 x + 3,\\qquad y=1 - 4 x,\\qquad y=5 - 4 x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x + y,\\,v=4 x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=- 2 x + y$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=2 x & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=0 & ⟹\\, & u=0 \\\\  & y=2 x + 3 & ⟹\\, & - 2 x + y=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4 x + y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=5 - 4 x & ⟹\\, & 4 x + y=5 & ⟹\\, & v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/d6/d605500af69b1a828496810a93a084b065ec92ebd99b03cbf14a37b03840dd31.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|- \\frac{1}{6}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550328 :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e9a8c7db3d0a811ca1dc95db8060a5488b4b9b2fb47fc08d25f1a959357626e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{13}{3}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{23}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{13}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{23}{4}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{169}{9}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{529}{16}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{169}+\\frac{16{y}^{2}}{529} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550331 :question/problem "![](images/50/50782efd1f5f15904e974810f5db14eb41ffa9a81f790226cd5048dc33c77a93.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{4}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{21}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{21}{4}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{16}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{441}{16}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{16{y}^{2}}{441} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550333 :question/problem "![](images/c6/c61e776c1c576b156b7fae95815459ee91b36cffd3b02ac1610a8293d9d08309.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{25}{4}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{22}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{22}{3}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{625}{16}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{484}{9}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{625}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{484} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550335 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b629b57b84284cff9d54d9ac47b886be8e77c80cb1cdd3c010bf92dc16dd274.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{10}{3}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{19}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{19}{3}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{100}{9}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{361}{9}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{361} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
